# Automatic Table of Contents in LaTeX

Here's a complete example showing how to generate an automatic table of contents:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

% Optional: Customize TOC depth
\setcounter{tocdepth}{3}  % Shows sections, subsections, and subsubsections

\begin{document}

% Generate the table of contents
\tableofcontents
\newpage  % Start content on a new page

% Your document content
\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section.

\subsection{Background}
Some background information.

\subsection{Motivation}
Why this topic matters.

\section{Methodology}
Description of methods used.

\subsection{Data Collection}
How data was gathered.

\subsubsection{Survey Design}
Details about the survey.

\subsection{Analysis}
How data was analyzed.

\section{Results}
The findings of the study.

\section{Conclusion}
Final thoughts and summary.

\end{document}
```

## Key Commands:

- **`\tableofcontents`** - Generates the TOC automatically based on sectioning commands
- **`\setcounter{tocdepth}{n}`** - Controls depth (0=chapters, 1=sections, 2=subsections, 3=subsubsections)
- **`\newpage`** - Optional, creates page break after TOC

## Important Notes:

1. **Compile twice**: LaTeX needs two compilation runs to generate the TOC correctly
2. Sectioning commands (`\section`, `\subsection`, etc.) are automatically included
3. Use `\section*{}` to create sections that won't appear in the TOC
4. Page numbers are added automatically

