# Solution for Power of Three

I'll provide multiple approaches to solve this problem:

## Approach 1: Iterative Division (With Loop)
```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }

        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(log₃n)  
**Space Complexity:** O(1)

## Approach 2: Recursive Solution
```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        if (n == 1) {
            return true;
        }
        if (n % 3 != 0) {
            return false;
        }
        return isPowerOfThree(n / 3);
    }
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(log₃n)  
**Space Complexity:** O(log₃n) due to recursion stack

## Approach 3: Mathematical Solution (No Loops/Recursion) ⭐
```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // Maximum power of 3 that fits in a 32-bit signed integer
        // 3^19 = 1162261467
        // 3^20 = 3486784401 > Integer.MAX_VALUE

        return n > 0 && 1162261467 % n == 0;
    }
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(1)  
**Space Complexity:** O(1)

**Explanation:** Since 3 is a prime number, the only divisors of 3^19 are powers of 3. If n is a power of 3 and within the integer range, then 3^19 must be divisible by n.

## Approach 4: Logarithm Solution (No Loops/Recursion)
```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // Calculate log base 3 of n
        // If n = 3^x, then log₃(n) = x (should be an integer)
        double logResult = Math.log10(n) / Math.log10(3);

        // Check if the result is close to an integer
        return Math.abs(logResult - Math.round(logResult)) < 1e-10;
    }
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(1)  
**Space Complexity:** O(1)

**Note:** This approach may have precision issues with floating-point arithmetic.

## Recommended Solution

For the **follow-up requirement (no loops/recursion)**, **Approach 3** is the best solution:

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        return n > 0 && 1162261467 % n == 0;
    }
}
```

This is elegant, efficient, and avoids floating-point precision issues. It leverages the mathematical property that all powers of 3 within the integer range must be divisors of the maximum power of 3 (3^19) that fits in a 32-bit integer.

